# v5.4.2-26 r2 plan — after round 1 (Opus, Fable, Codex: all "not yet")

**Date:** 2026-11-27. Reviews: `tests/v5.4.2-26/review/{opus,fable,codex}-r1.md`. Codex's six probes were run by the author on fresh clones of frozen r1 (`codex-r1-probes/*.author-run.txt`); every outcome matched Codex's prediction. Probe 02's COPY step was refused by row security (0A000, as for every COPY into an RLS table), and the probe's data lines then failed as SQL; that leg proves nothing either way.

## Round 1 reviewed

All three: **not yet**. Battery 202/202 and races 11/11 were reproduced by Opus and Fable on their own clones. The earlier patches' invariant functions all pass with -26 applied. Statement shapes and the earlier owner roles (tally_seal, tally_close, tally_void, tally_dispute) found no path -26 refuses that they take.

### Blocking (to fix in r2)

| # | Finding | Found by | How |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | An execution under **another customer's** execute decision: a visit's decision link carries no customer, and `collections_act_check` compares location and meter only. Once a location changes customer (tally_app can), the old customer's order is executed under the new customer's decision. | Opus B1, Codex B1 (probe 01, executed) | Compare the decision's customer with the order's in the visit guard, and make the visit's decision key `(id, tenant_id, customer)` through the order. Test the transfer case, with a blocked decision for the old customer. |
| 2 | A **location-wide blocked** decision does not stop a **meter-level eligible** one: the two are separate subjects, both live; order, execution and notice go through (a medical hold ignored). | Opus B2; Fable note | Fail toward protection: an act is refused while any live non-eligible decision covers its subject (the location-wide one covers every meter there). The act check takes both subjects' locks, location-wide first (lock-order rule amended). Battery O7 is rewritten: the subjects are not independent. |
| 3 | Under **REPEATABLE READ** an order is cancelled after its execution committed: the order's status update has no isolation pin and reads visits from a stale snapshot. | Opus B3, Fable B1 (two sessions, executed), Codex B3 | The disconnect-order guard refuses a status update outside READ COMMITTED (25000), as every subject-locked write does; the visit keeps its `FOR SHARE`. Race CR8c at REPEATABLE READ and SERIALIZABLE. |
| 4 | **Multi-row dunning inserts** freeze a wrong `previous_stage`: the BEFORE ROW trigger reads the projection the AFTER ROW trigger has not yet moved for the earlier row of the same statement. | Codex B2 (probe 02, executed) | Read the bill's latest stage-moving event under the bill's row lock (the events are the authority, the projection follows them), not the projection. Battery: VALUES of two, INSERT … SELECT, a writable CTE. |
| 5 | **User links not tied to the utility** (`created_by`, `approved_by` → `users(id)` only), and a platform administrator approves under their own row with no impersonation. Against R-12 (2026-11-05) and the Tally User ruling (2026-11-07). | Opus B4, Fable S4, Codex S2 (probe 04, executed) | Two-column keys `(user, tenant_id)` against users, as -25 did; the approval guard requires a supervisor of the order's utility (the impersonated Tally User for staff). Check how -25 keys its actor links and follow it. |

### Should fix (in r2)

| # | Finding | Found by | Plan |
|---|---|---|---|
| 6 | **A repeat late fee or reminder moves the stage backwards** (shutoff warning → late fee assessed; even a reminder dated 2020). Ryan 2026-11-22 makes a monthly penalty normal at Pecan Flat. | Opus S1, Fable S1, Codex N2 (probe 03) | **Settled by rule** (the stage is a status the database derives, not where money goes): the escalation steps move the stage only forward along the column's own ladder, current → reminder → late fee → shutoff warning → scheduled → disconnected → collections; a repeat step on a bill already past it logs with `new_stage = previous_stage`. Payment plan, resolved and written off move as now. Battery: three monthly fees on one bill, one after a shutoff warning. Fable asked whether this is Ryan's call; it is reversible wording plus one function, so it goes in by rule and Ryan can overrule. |
| 7 | **The gate keys on one label of a late fee**: a late fee recorded as a `penalty` charge or as a `late_fee` bill line needs no decision. | Opus S2, Fable note | Gate by what the charge is (every charge type that is a late penalty, and the bill line type), per `guard-the-act-not-the-label`; or refuse the other labels for a late penalty. Inspect -23's and -30's line types first. |
| 8 | **Surplus approval links** escape the order and kind checks: a matched visit with evidence can name another order's approvals. | Codex S1 (probe 01), Fable S6 | Validate every non-null approval link (own order, own kind), or refuse a link that is not needed. |
| 9 | **An execution backdated** before its order existed, under an old decision. | Opus S3 | A visit's arrival is no earlier than its order's creation and falls on its execute decision's day. |
| 10 | **Replica-mode history load** leaves `do_not_disconnect` false under a protective enrollment, and the fence blocks repair. | Opus S4, Fable S5, Codex S4 (probe 03) | Make the recompute trigger `ENABLE ALWAYS` (as the fence is), so a load recomputes; test in replica mode. |
| 11 | **No invariant function**, and the closing block skips three existing ones. | Opus S5 | Add `assert_collections_guard_invariants` (every guard trigger present, enabled ALWAYS, the fences, the grants) and call the three. |
| 12 | **A voided bill counts as issued**, and acts never recheck their bill. | Opus S6 | Decisions and acts refuse a void bill (read with -23's void state); test with a bill voided through `void_invoice()`. |
| 13 | **Blank captured values** pass an execution under a supervisor approval. | Fable S3 | The captured serial and service point need an alphanumeric character (`blank-checks-whitelist-alnum`). |
| 14 | **A disconnection can be logged on one bill only** (one line per decision), and on any bill of the customer. | Fable S2, Opus N1 | One log line per decision and bill, for bills of the decision's customer; the late fee keeps one bill. |
| 15 | **Race verdicts and mutation catches** print the same FAIL for a timeout as for damage. | Codex S3 | Apply `race-catch-names-its-damage`: each race leg names the damage it read back; the runner checks the reason. |

### Notes taken into r2 as words

- **R1 is larger than stated** (Fable S7): an execute decision written on a Texas evening is dated the next UTC day and covers that day's work. The schema holds no time zone per utility, so R1 is restated and the core gets a contract at the mirror (an execute decision is written on the utility's local day of the visit, after that day's weather check). `adhoc_charges.effective_date` defaults to the session's day: one sentence in the contract.
- Opus N2 (an eligible disconnect for a `do_not_disconnect` customer is admitted), N3 (an operator ends an enrollment to lift protection), N4 (credit reporting not gated): the core's evaluation and wave 5/7's scope; listed in the header.
- Fable reported multi-row and writable-CTE inserts clean; Codex's probe 02, run by the author, shows the second row of a two-row insert recording `previous_stage = current` where it should read `reminder_sent`. The probe stands (item 4); Fable's shapes did not chain two stage-moving rows on one bill.
- Fable notes: a `late_fee_applied` line is admitted after its charge was voided (r2: the line needs a live charge); ungated steps such as `resolved` move the stage with no backing fact (the core's, stated in the header).
- Remaining notes and test gaps: each review's N section; the r2 battery adds a test for every finding above.

## Next

Build r2, tests first, then battery, races, mutations, ci.sh; freeze; review with all three.

## As built (r2, 2026-11-27)

Every item above is in the patch; its header lists them ("r2 (round 1's findings)") and the residuals R1, R6, R7 carry the notes. Choices made in the build, by rule:

- **Item 2's lock.** Rather than take several subject locks in an order, the subject's advisory lock no longer includes the meter: every subject at one location (the location-wide one and each meter's) shares one lock, so an act reads all their live decisions with none in flight, and no second lock order is needed. Contention is per customer, act, bill and location, which is negligible.
- **Item 2's reach.** The check binds only the act itself (`p_live`), not a log line: a disconnection already carried out is still logged.
- **Item 4.** `dunning_events.record_seq` (the shared record sequence) orders a bill's steps; the stage a step moves from is the latest stage-moving step's, read under the bill's row lock. A BEFORE trigger sees the rows an earlier part of its own statement inserted, which is what makes a multi-row insert correct.
- **Item 5.** The approver must be an active `tenant_admin` of the order's own utility. `session_is_supervisor()` (-13) is unchanged, since other gates use it; -26 no longer calls it. A T1 supervisor writing an approval for T2 is now refused by this check (42501, `[F3a]`) before row security, reading nothing of T2's (battery H3 updated).
- **Item 6.** From a stage off the ladder (payment plan, resolved, written off) an escalation step moves the bill to its own stage: collections resumed. Battery A8c and A8h now expect the bill to stay where it was.
- **Item 7.** A late fee is gated under `late_fee` and `penalty`; a `late_fee` or `penalty` bill line must name a gated charge of the bill's customer; a charge cannot become or stop being a late fee. The precondition also stops on such charges and lines. `battery-21`'s late-fee line (A5) now bills a gated charge, so `battery-21` needs -26 (as `ci.sh` runs it).
- **Item 9.** An execution is not dated before its order or its decision was recorded, nor in the future. A battery transaction stamps everything at one instant, so the battery's executions now arrive at `now()` under decisions for today; the races likewise.
- **Item 11.** `assert_collections_guard_invariants()` pins -26's triggers (ENABLE ALWAYS), constraints, unique indexes, table grants and row security, and its functions' bodies, settings and grants; `tests/v5.4.2-26/fill-manifest.py` writes the lists. The patch's last block calls it and every earlier patch's.
- **Item 15 (Codex S3).** The mutation runner no longer counts a race leg that failed for want of a lock wait, or because session A failed, as a catch.

**Not done, and why:** the "decision recorded before the act" bound (Opus S3) is applied to executions only. For a charge's or a step's date it would refuse every history load and every battery fixture dated before the transaction, and C3h's reason (no relying on an old evaluation at execution) is about executions.

