27:## 2026-11-25 (scenarios, last) — Ryan: when a credit is netted against a bounce is a utility setting, default at the bounce
29:- **When a customer's credit is applied to another payment's bounce shortfall is a per-utility setting: "at the bounce" (the platform default) or "at a refund" (when a refund of a credit is asked for) (Ryan, 2026-11-25: "shouldn't this be configurable in the app?", then "yes, default at the bounce").** The customer sees one net figure from the bounce either way (Kyle 10-30, who left the tracking underneath to Ryan). The per-state rule on deducting a debt before reporting unclaimed money (Ryan 11-02) stays its own row and matters only under "at a refund". *Why the default:* it matches Kyle's words ("use up whatever is in the total credit"), and the customer's balance can never jump later (from $10 to $30 at escheat in SCN-0103) through no act of theirs; at the bounce the credit is not yet unclaimed, so applying it is an ordinary use of the customer's own money against the customer's own debt (this stream's reading; counsel confirms with the unclaimed-money questions). *Why configurable:* a utility may want each credit kept intact until the customer spends or refunds it; that is its own policy, stated at onboarding. *Not chosen:* "at a refund" only (the scenarios' earlier reading), or "at the bounce" with no setting. *Migration cost:* additive, one utility setting (the money stream's); the netting record (v5.4.2-27) is unchanged, only when the core writes it. *Where it lives:* `scenarios/cash.md` SCN-0087, SCN-0103; board note to money.
31:## 2026-11-25 (scenarios, latest) — Ryan: Kyle's questions are triaged before they are sent
33:- **The five unsent batches for Kyle (83 questions) are cut to 28 and five figure checks (Ryan, 2026-11-25: "go with recommendation").** A point a standing rule decides is settled by that rule and recorded in its scenario as "Settled by rule (2026-11-25, triage of Kyle's questions)", naming the rule. The rules: follow the tariff, with the platform default only where it is silent, so a point a utility can state becomes an onboarding setting (Kyle 11-03); the settings the cut-off checks need are stated before go-live (Ryan 2026-11-25); a missing rule never holds back a refund, and a city's undercharge reaches back six months (Kyle 11-03 answer 8; 11-05 answers 10–12); bookkeeping, record shapes and report layouts are the stream's; an existing ruling or built behaviour that is right for money stands. Kyle keeps what could make an amount wrong, readings of law or contract terms, industry practice that sets a default affecting money, and conflicts between rulings; where unsure, a question stayed with him. *Why:* the batches had grown by turning every open point into a question, which buries the ones that matter. *Not chosen:* sending all 83 in order. *Two points moved to Ryan* (netting a credit against a bounce: at the bounce or at the refund; SCN-0087, SCN-0103; Kyle 10-30 left it to Ryan) and *one to counsel* (when Texas's three years start for returned unclaimed money; SCN-0119). *Extension to note:* each city also states before go-live whether an estimated bill can ground a cut-off (the go-live rule applied to estimates; SCN-0151). *Where it lives:* the five briefs (`application/kyle-brief-2026-11-2*-scenario-batch-*.md`), the scenarios' "Settled by rule" notes, `application/roadmap/decisions.json` (K-14, K-16, K-18, K-19, K-20).
35:## 2026-11-25 (scenarios, later) — Ryan: the gas cost, zones and reads scenarios are written now, ahead of their patches
37:- **The gas cost, zones and reads family is written before the rates stream's patches for it exist (Ryan, 2026-11-25: "start the gas cost, zones and reads scenarios").** This departs from the 2026-10-29 entry below, which put gas cost, zones and reads after their patches mirror. *Why:* none of those patches is claimed yet, so waiting would leave the scenarios stream idle; written now, the scenarios become the patches' specification, and Kyle checks the figures before the schema is shaped. *Not chosen:* waiting for the patches (no date for them), or another family first (the handoff recommended this one). *The risk:* a patch may shape things differently; each scenario states what is not built and writes to the ruling, as the README's convention for schema still being built requires. *Migration cost:* none (no schema). *Where it lives:* `scenarios/gas-cost-zones-and-reads.md` (SCN-0130 – 0145), its header.
39:## 2026-11-25 (scenarios) — Ryan: the cut-off checks' settings are required before go-live; a pledge carries the day it was received
41:- **A pledge carries the day the utility received it, entered by the operator, beside the database's stamp of when it was keyed (Ryan, 2026-11-25: "you just enter the date it was received").** The date is no earlier than the agency's date on the pledge, no later than the day it is keyed, and written once. The rule that turns on receipt reads it: 16 TAC §7.460(b)(2) protects a delinquent residential customer "for a billing period in which the provider receives a written pledge", and binds investor-owned utilities, not city systems. *Why:* today the only receipt the record holds is the keying stamp, so a letter that arrived Sep 30 and was keyed Oct 1 (or Nov 1) reads as received in the wrong billing period, and re-recording a pledge (a phone pledge replaced by its letter; a pledge entered again once its agreement is on file) moves the date again. *Not chosen:* the keying stamp alone with same-day keying required of the core (a late keying would silently shift the legal protection). The stamp stays as the audit trail; the bounds keep any backdating small, the same pattern as a payment's customer's date. *Migration cost:* additive, one column on the pledges table (money's, -24); nothing changes meaning. *Where it lives:* `scenarios/unmatched-cash-and-pledges.md` SCN-0124, SCN-0128; board note to money.
43:- **A utility cannot finish onboarding until it has stated the values the collections checks read: the service level for held money, the retention period for held money, and a pledge rule (which may be "a pledge gives no protection"). The database's fail-safe stays: a check that cannot run blocks the act (Ryan, 2026-11-25: "Require them at go-live, option 3").** *Why:* a missing value today makes the aged-receipt list refuse to run, and -26 treats a check that cannot run as blocking, so a utility that skipped one setting would find every cut-off stopped without warning (SCN-0120 (c)). Treating silence as "no protection" instead would cut off a customer whose money sits unidentified, the failure CI-056 exists to prevent. Requiring the values at go-live keeps the protective behaviour as a safety net that a live utility never meets, and fits the standing rule that a utility states its own regulated values with no platform default (K-07). *Not chosen:* block until stated (option 1: safe, but stops collections at a live utility by surprise); silence is no protection (option 2). *Migration cost:* none in the schema; an onboarding step for the core. The settings stay nullable in the database, so a utility mid-onboarding is not refused. *Where it lives:* `scenarios/unmatched-cash-and-pledges.md` SCN-0120 and SCN-0124 (d); board note to money.
51:## 2026-11-22 (-30) — Ryan: -30 mirrored, ahead of money's pending patches
53:- **-30 mirrored into `tu.sql` ahead of -25, -26 and -24 (Ryan, 2026-11-22: "mirror it once money's reviewers are done").** *Why:* -30 was ready from all three reviewers (r5; r6 words only) and shares no function or trigger with the three, which strict-apply over it and whose batteries pass; waiting meant retesting it after each of theirs. *The cost:* money re-runs its batteries on the rebuilt `tally`. The rebuild waited for money's round-10 reviewers and its suite run. → DEPLOY-VERIFICATION.
56:## 2026-11-22 (rates, -30 r4) — the plain read of a premise's places reads what is committed; round-4 findings settled by standing rule
62:## 2026-11-22 (scenarios) — Ryan: the amount the utility's error hid is one charge, part of it due later, not a matched pair of entries
64:- **A correction of a bill the utility's error made too low is charged at its full true amount; a record says the hidden part is due with the next regular bill (Ryan, 2026-11-22: "go with option A"; A of two).** Example (SCN-0033 b): charge $79.06, $13.23 of it due Dec 31, $65.83 due Dec 1; a clerk on Dec 5 quotes $85.65 ($6.59 of it the penalty), of which $13.23 is not yet due. The next bill shows the $13.23 and does not charge it again. Paying it early is not an overpayment; an account that closes still owes it; voiding the correction reverses all of it. **Replaces the matched pair of the entry "a corrected bill shows the full true figure" (below); what the bill shows there stands, with "due with your next bill" as a label instead of an offsetting amount.** *Why:* the money is owed once the correction is issued and only its due date differs; one charge cannot be counted or taken twice, and the revenue stays with the correction. *Why not the pair* (−$13.23 and +$13.23, linked as something other than a reversal): the schema counts positive lines only, so the correction would read open after it is paid; an early payoff would read as an overpayment; a close before the next bill and a void would each need a rule of their own. *Cost:* a new record, "part of this bill is due later", read by the penalty rule and the aging; Kyle's answer 2 (2026-11-05) said one due date per bill and no schema change, so he is told (K-14). *This stream's readings, not ruled:* a payment pays what is due before what is not yet due; the same record can carry the day-60 disputed amount (which then needs no pair either). *Settled by rule after the re-review (2026-11-22, Opus and Fable):* the record is per line (the true line less the voided line), since the payment order would otherwise leave $12.45 of priced lines open and contradict the $13.10 base Ryan kept; the next bill prints the amount and holds no line for it, since a line would take the money a second time, against "cannot be counted or taken twice"; "not yet due" is its own row in the aging. Kyle is asked about each (SCN-0033's Opens). *Where it lives:* SCN-0033, 0024, 0089; the board (scenarios → money).
66:## 2026-11-22 (rates, -30 r3) — a past read refuses a place with two different answers and gives one answer for the same place twice; round-3 findings settled by standing rule
70:- *Correction (2026-11-22, -30 r5 review, Opus S1, executed):* settled is not right. Where a successor's writer began before its predecessor's void, every read at that instant after both finish answers both (or refuses), though a read made at the instant answered one. So waiting for the writers is no remedy; the instant must be one when no transaction begun earlier is still open. A billing run's one instant (2026-11-05, R-22) must be chosen so: a rule for the core. The R-22 ruling is unchanged. → -30 r6 (words only), L7; board.
73:## 2026-11-22 (scenarios) — Ryan: a corrected bill shows the full true figure; what the utility's error hid is moved to the next bill by a matched pair of lines
75:- **When the utility's error made a bill too low and it is voided and rebilled, the correction lists all the true lines (SCN-0033 b: 7.5 Mcf, $79.06), then a line moving the hidden extra to the next bill (−$13.23), leaving the original amount ($65.83) due; the next regular bill carries +$13.23 (Ryan, 2026-11-22: "go with option B"; B of two; Kyle's batch-2 answer 2, how it is shown).** The two lines are equal and opposite: not revenue, not taxed, no usage. The correction holds the revenue, the tax and the usage. The carried amount's penalty base is its tariff-priced part only ($13.10). *Why:* the correction shows the true read, usage, rates and tax; Kyle's answer 1 ("the lower of the original (wrong) bill and the corrected bill") implies the corrected bill has its own full amount. *Why not* the lower figure ($65.83, with $13.23 as new December charges; the stream's earlier reading): it repeats the wrong bill, no bill ever shows the true November, and usage and tax fall on the wrong bill. *This stream's readings, not ruled:* the moved line takes the correction's age in the aging report (SCN-0024); a moved line with no carried line by the next regular bill is an error to surface; the same pair can carry a disputed amount at day 60 (entry below). *Schema:* the mirrored schema allows an `adjustment` line and ledger entry; nothing found that pairs two such lines, so the core does it or a later money patch pairs them (money to weigh). *Correction (2026-11-22, review round 4, Opus and Fable; confirmed on `tally`):* the schema's open amount counts positive lines only, so the correction reads $13.23 open after $65.83 is paid and would take it twice; and a ledger row naming the correction's charge is a reversal of it (-23, X1), against "the correction holds the revenue". How the $13.23 is recorded was put back to Ryan and settled the same day as one charge, part of it due later (entry above); what the bill shows stands. *To Kyle (K-14):* the figures. *Where it lives:* `scenarios/cancel-rebill.md` SCN-0033; `scenarios/periods-and-aging.md` SCN-0024; the board (scenarios → money).
77:## 2026-11-22 (scenarios) — Ryan: on the full-amount penalty base, whether an unpaid bill takes the penalty once or every month is the city's own setting, with no default; Pecan Flat takes it every month
79:- **Pecan Flat's late penalty is 10% of the full amount owed, excluding earlier penalties (Kyle's flag 4 of batch 2, confirmed). A utility on that base must also state whether an unpaid bill is penalized once or each month it stays unpaid; there is no default, and onboarding asks (Ryan, 2026-11-22: "go with option C"; C of three). Pecan Flat's answer is every month: $21.58 on Mar 1, with January's $95.00 penalized in February and again in March.** *Why:* Kyle's answers (11-03, 3; 11-05, 4) settle the base and that penalties never compound, but neither says the same principal may be penalized repeatedly; that is read from "amount due" only, and a real city should state what it does. *Why not* the every-month reading built in (inferred from two words of one ordinance); *nor* once per bill built in (in this example the same $12.08 as the platform default, which Kyle ruled this tariff overrides). *Unchanged:* the platform default base (current bill only) is charged once. *Cost:* one more field in the late-penalty rules, not yet built (R-11, with -26 or after). *To Kyle (K-14):* was the repeat intended, and should tariff counsel check a 10% charge repeated monthly (it mostly reaches customers whose cut-off is held back: weather, medical, payment plans); whether Texas law limits it for a city is not checked. *Where it lives:* `scenarios/billing-pipeline-and-charge-composition.md` SCN-0053; `scenarios/cast.md` §1; the board (scenarios → money).
81:## 2026-11-22 (money, -25) — Ryan: the silent-city dispute default stops the late fee and both disconnection steps
83:- **At a city-owned system with no dispute rule of its own, the default blocks three things on the disputed amount: the late fee, ordering a disconnection and carrying one out (Ryan, 2026-11-22: "go with option 2, late fee and both disconnection steps"; option 2 of three).** Not blocked: a disconnection notice, referral to collections, credit reporting. The city may still disconnect over the undisputed rest, and the protection lasts at most the 60 days. *Why:* the same blocks as the Texas row the default copies (16 TAC §7.45(6)(D) as drafted; its late fee is our reading); both real city policies in hand (Brenham, CPS Energy) stop both disconnection steps; cutting off service over an amount in dispute is the worse harm. *Why not the late fee only:* a silent city could cut off service over an amount it may not penalize; departs from CI-074. *Why not all six:* wider than the Texas rule that binds larger utilities, and than either city's policy. *Settled with it, as stated to Ryan:* the default's review stages and utility duties are empty (no ruling sets them). *Known departure, to Kyle (K-14):* CI-074 also bars dunning notices on the disputed amount. *To counsel:* whether *Memphis Light v. Craft* (1978) reaches a Texas city's customers; it requires a chance to protest before a cutoff, and does not itself freeze the amount. *Cost of changing later:* a new dated row. *Where it lives:* `application/wave3-build-2026-10-18/v5.4.2-25-r2-plan.md` item 9 design; the board.
85:## 2026-11-22 (money, -25) — Ryan: the silent-city dispute default reaches every customer
87:- **The platform default for a city-owned system with no dispute rule of its own reaches every customer class, not only residential and small commercial (Ryan, 2026-11-22: "go with option 1, every customer"; option 1 of two).** *Why:* Kyle's answer 1 (2026-11-05) says "the customer's dispute" and names no class; CI-054 and CI-074 limit the protection by amount and grounds, not by class; the disputed-amount limit and the 60-day end already close off abuse. *Why not the Texas classes* (16 TAC §7.45 protects residential and small commercial): that line marks whom the regulator's consumer rule covers, and the rule does not bind a city; it would penalize a large customer on an amount still contested because the city wrote nothing down. *Known cost:* a silent city holds back a larger penalty on a large customer's dispute than §7.45 makes an investor-owned utility hold back. *Not checked:* any city tariff that treats large customers' disputes differently. *Unchanged:* an investor-owned or cooperative system's unprotected customers get no shelter from the law row. *Cost of changing later:* new dated rows; a dispute already determined keeps the row it cites. *The penalty when the 60 days end unresolved* was settled the same day in the scenarios session (entry below). *Where it lives:* `application/wave3-build-2026-10-18/v5.4.2-25-r2-plan.md` item 9; APPLICATION-CONTRACTS AC-46; the board.
89:## 2026-11-22 (scenarios) — Ryan: when the silent-city default's 60 days end with the dispute unresolved, the disputed amount moves to the next bill's due date
91:- **At a city with no dispute rule of its own, when a dispute outlasts the 60 days, the disputed amount still unpaid is shown on the next regular bill and takes that bill's due date; a late penalty posts on it only if that date passes unpaid. The dispute stays open, and if the utility later decides for the customer the amount and any penalty on it are reversed (Ryan, 2026-11-22: "go with option B"; B of four).** *Why:* keeps the 60-day limit, and gives the customer a bill and a date to pay by before any penalty; follows Kyle's answer 2 (2026-11-05) for an amount the utility's own error hid. *Why not* a penalty on day 60 (no notice, and it may land because the city was slow); *nor* holding it until resolution (fits CI-054 and CI-074 fully, but removes the 60-day limit for penalties: a forgotten dispute holds it off indefinitely); *nor* never penalizing that amount on that bill (rewards keeping a dispute open). *Known cost, stated to Ryan:* a penalty can still land on an amount under open dispute, which CI-054 and CI-074 protect until resolution; to Kyle (K-14). *This stream's readings, not ruled:* the next regular bill is the first one issued after the 60th day; the amount is carried for its due date, not billed again (no second charge, no new tax); it keeps its original age in the aging report (what was owed keeps its age, Kyle 2026-11-03, answer 10). *Schema:* none known; the penalty engine's act (core). *Where it lives:* `scenarios/cancel-rebill.md` SCN-0033; the board (scenarios → money, AC-46).
92:- *Correction (2026-11-22, same session):* the example put to Ryan said protection ends Jan 15 (bill issued Nov 16); the last protected day is Jan 14, because the Texas rule the default copies counts the issue day as day 1 (16 TAC §7.45(6)(D)(ii), read that day).
94:## 2026-11-22 (money, -25) — Ryan: of the two dispute rulings of 2026-11-07, the scenarios version stands
96:- **A city's own dispute rule wins; where Texas law binds, the law row governs; only a city-owned system with no dispute rule of its own gets a platform default. The default takes a dispute raised before the bill's due date, protects the disputed amount only (the whole bill when no amount is recorded), and ends at the earlier of the dispute's resolution and 60 days after issue (Ryan, 2026-11-22: "go with your recommendation, the scenarios version"; both rulings laid side by side for the first time).** The "(money, -25 r1 review)" ruling of 2026-11-07 yields (its correction below); the three "(scenarios)" dispute entries of that day stand. *Why a city's own rule wins:* Kyle's standing rule (2026-11-03, repeated 2026-11-05): always follow the tariff, platform defaults only where it is silent; a platform rule over every customer would override city tariffs. *Why the disputed amount only:* invariants CI-054 and CI-074 both protect the disputed amount, and CI-074 names the harm of suspending everything (customers "indefinitely defer collectable charges by manufacturing nominal disputes"). Neither 11-07 entry cited the invariants or the standing rule. *Why the 60 days (the closest call):* it copies 16 TAC §7.45(6)(D), as Kyle's six-month backbilling default for silent cities copies Texas's, and no one has to remember to close a dispute. *Known costs, stated to Ryan:* a slow utility can run out the 60 days; one reason first given for them (most cities would rely on the default) was found unsupported; a penalty on a disputed amount at a Brenham-style city ("the disputed bill should still be paid", silent on penalties as such) departs from CI-054, which exempts disputed amounts without a carve-out, so it goes to Kyle (K-14). *Amends K-07 for disputes:* the 2026-10-18 entry's "a city with no tariff row has no dispute terms" gives way to the default. *Schema:* -25 r2 needs a platform default dispute row, and its "no terms" outcome revisited, before it freezes (r2 plan item 9). *Still open (Ryan):* the penalty on the disputed amount when the 60 days end unresolved; which customer classes the default reaches. *Where it lives:* APPLICATION-CONTRACTS AC-46; `application/wave3-build-2026-10-18/v5.4.2-25-r2-plan.md` item 9; SCN-0033 (already states it); the board.
97:- *Same answer given in the scenarios session the same day (Ryan, 2026-11-22: "option 1").* That session's comparison did not show Ryan the two invariants. The two points listed as still open are settled the same day (entries above). *Note:* the 60-day end also departs from CI-054 and CI-074 when a dispute outlasts it (both protect the amount until resolution, and CI-054 does not list the dispute exemption as configurable); it goes to Kyle with the Brenham question (K-14; SCN-0033, Open).
116:## 2026-11-07 (scenarios) — Ryan: every utility has a hidden Tally User; Tally staff act in a utility by impersonating it
118:- **Each utility gets one Tally User when it is set up: a user of that utility, hidden from the utility's user management, whose permissions Tally sets (supervisor acts and user administration included). A Tally employee acts in a utility by impersonating its Tally User through a recorded session (Ryan, 2026-11-07: "Go ahead, record it as described"; Ryan's own proposal; roadmap R-27, which it closes).** Four conditions, proposed with it and accepted: (1) the Tally User has no login of its own and is reachable only through an impersonation session, so every act names a real Tally employee (the session record: which employee, start, end, a required reason; every change marked with the session by the database); (2) it is hidden from the utility's user list, never from its records: the utility's change history shows "Tally (employee, reason)" on every change made through it; (3) the utility cannot change it: one per utility, created by the database at setup, a second refused, a login bound to it refused, and only Tally may change its permissions or switch it; (4) it does not count as one of the utility's own staff, so a rule about the utility's supervisors (who is the last active one) or a count of its users leaves it out. *Why:* Ryan wants Tally's support staff to have as much freedom as possible to get things done. The two-column keys (R-12, same day as the impersonation ruling) need every "who did it" stamp on a utility's record to name a user of that utility, and a Tally employee's own user row belongs to Tally; the Tally User is a user of the utility, so the keys hold while its powers are Tally's, not those of whichever utility user would otherwise be impersonated. It also answers who sets up a new utility's first supervisor and restores one after the last is switched off or leaves: the Tally User can. *Why not* (a) a session capped at the impersonated user's permissions (Nov 5's proposal): friction for support, and no one to impersonate when a utility has no active supervisor; (b) an exception letting Tally staff do user administration as themselves (the stream's earlier option 1 for R-27): needless once the Tally User exists; (c) the last supervisor barred from switching itself off plus a provisioning service account (option 2): a write-everywhere credential, and no answer for a supervisor who has left; (d) Tally staff writing as themselves: the keys would refuse their stamps unless each employee had a user row in every utility. *Unchanged from Nov 5:* Tally decides alone; a session needs a reason; the utility sees every session and its changes; a platform administrator cannot be impersonated; impersonating a named user of the utility (with that user's permissions) remains allowed; a Tally employee reads across utilities without a session (Q-11). *Not built:* the Tally User, its guards, the session record and the per-change marking; no stream owns `users` yet (board, Unassigned). *Where it lives:* `scenarios/tenant-isolation.md` SCN-0062, SCN-0065, SCN-0068, SCN-0080; roadmap R-27.
123:- *Correction (2026-11-22):* this ruling yields to the scenarios stream's of the same day (entry of 2026-11-22 above). It was asked without those in front of Ryan. Not a platform rule for every customer; not the whole bill; not until resolution without an end.
192:## 2026-11-05 (scenarios) — Ryan: every link between two utility tables proves both rows belong to the same utility (R-12, option 1)
194:- **The cross-tenant link fix (CI-116) ties every foreign key between two tables that carry a tenant to the tenant, as a two-column key (the link's id plus the row's own `tenant_id`, against the target's `UNIQUE (id, tenant_id)`) (Ryan, 2026-11-05; roadmap R-12; option 1 of two).** Counted on `tally` 2026-11-05 (every mirrored patch, -23 and -28 included): **273** single-column foreign keys between tenant tables are not tied to the tenant (the measurement's 267 was before the latest mirrors), on 82 tables, pointing at 43 (21 of them self-references); 86 are already tied; 18 of the 43 targets need the new unique key. By target: `users` 117, `customers` 24, `meters` 16, `invoices` 14, `service_locations` 10, `meter_readings` and `billing_runs` 8 each. Unmirrored patches (-25, -26, -29) may add more; the patch recounts. *Why:* Postgres checks a foreign key without row-level security, and ids are global, so today a session can point a row at another utility's record it cannot read (an application bug, a tampered request, a leaked id, or a cross-utility session); the database is the only guard that catches it. Two-column keys reuse the `tenant_id` every row already carries, so the application (raw SQL over Dapper, decided 2026-06-07) writes nothing new and its queries do not change. A CI check can then refuse any new untied link, so the rule holds as the schema grows. *Why not option 2 (same-tenant triggers on the links that carry weight):* every link, today's and each new one, would need a judgement of whether it matters, and a link judged harmless can become load-bearing; a weaker promise for a small saving (memory rule "design permanent"). *Made possible by* the impersonation ruling the same day: every "who did it" link names a user of the record's own utility, so the 117 user links are tied like the rest. *The one deliberate exception:* the impersonation session's link to the Tally employee, who is not a user of the utility. *Details for the patch:* a nullable link is still allowed empty (MATCH SIMPLE); an `ON DELETE SET NULL` rule names only the id column (Postgres 15+). *Where it lives:* the board ("After both schema streams finish"); `scenarios/tenant-isolation.md` SCN-0064; roadmap R-12.
198:- **Tally's own staff (the platform administrator role) may impersonate a utility's user and act on that user's behalf; Tally decides alone, without the utility's permission, and the utility can see every session afterwards (Ryan, 2026-11-05; option 1 of three: Tally alone, the utility grants each session, or a standing permission set at onboarding).** Built on the stream's proposal, not objected to: (1) the database writes an impersonation record (which Tally employee, which utility user, start, end, a required reason such as a support ticket) and refuses a session without a reason; (2) every change made in the session is traceable to that record, marked by the database, not by an application log; (3) the session has the impersonated user's permissions, never more, and a platform administrator cannot be impersonated; (4) a supervisor's act (closing a period, a write-off, binding a login) needs the utility's supervisor to be the one impersonated. A Tally employee's own access, not impersonating, is read-only across utilities (today the isolation check also lets a platform administrator write). *Why:* the records a regulator or court reads must say who really acted; under impersonation every change is by a user of the utility, visibly by Tally acting as them, which also lets every link between tables be tied to the utility, the 117 links to the acting user included (R-12; the user links would otherwise refuse a Tally employee, whose user row belongs to another tenant). *Why Tally alone:* fastest support, and the common pattern; the utility's protection is that every session is visible to it, with its reason. *Not built:* the impersonation record, the per-change marking, the read-only rule for plain platform access; no stream owns `users` yet (board, Unassigned). *Where it lives:* `scenarios/tenant-isolation.md` SCN-0065, SCN-0068; roadmap R-21.
199:- *Correction (2026-11-07, Ryan):* "a Tally employee's own access is read-only" and items (3) and (4) (the session capped at the impersonated user's permissions; a supervisor's act only by impersonating the utility's supervisor) were the stream's proposal, recorded as "not objected to", not Ryan's ruling; Ryan does not hold them. Replaced by the Tally User (entry of 2026-11-07, above): Tally acts in a utility through its Tally User, with permissions Tally sets. The rest of this entry stands.
1063:*Why:* Kyle's "closings of the record, not in-place edits" (R-12) read strictly. An in-place `effective_end` would make "what did we believe on date X about when this ended" wrong — exactly the reproduction failure the substrate exists to prevent. A revoked exemption is a new row (`status = 'revoked'`, `effective_end`, `revoked_*`) superseding the active one; `customer_tax_exemption_as_of()` still finds the exemption for dates inside the bracket, which is why revocation-as-succession is semantically right. Fable's H2 (a revoked row inserted *beside* the still-open active row kept the customer exempt) led to the exclusion constraint on `(customer_id, exemption_type, bracket)` over asserted open rows.
