55764:    'v5.4.2-20 (r3), v5.4.2-21, v5.4.2-22, v5.4.2-23 (tally_void executes the four ledger-tree helpers that its void and the reversal rule call), v5.4.2-25 r2 (tally_dispute, the owner of the dispute status move). Raises unless: tally_seal, tally_void, tally_close and tally_dispute exist, are NOLOGIN without SUPERUSER, BYPASSRLS, CREATEROLE, CREATEDB or REPLICATION, have no members and are members of nothing, hold and set no default privilege, can define no code, own exactly their pinned SECURITY DEFINER functions (by identity) and no relation, type or schema, and hold exactly their granted privileges, grant options included (seal_role_privileges); tally_core executes no definer but the two RLS helpers (by identity); tally_app, tally_core and PUBLIC hold no privilege on the receipts and no UPDATE on a header version; only tally_close writes the snapshots, the closed-through date and a period''s close (closed_at, closed_by; the application withdraws an open period by status); no public function names the old void setting (a lexical test); every guard of the seals exists exactly as defined (PostgreSQL 16''s rendering) and ENABLE ALWAYS; PUBLIC holds no privilege on any relation in public; and adhoc_charges_billed_names_its_bill is present and validated. Run in the patch tail and in CI.';
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""v5.4.2-26 — prove the -26 checks CATCH drift, not merely pass.

Each mutation alters a copy of the patch, applies it (strict: search_path ''
and function-body checking on) to a fresh clone of a base that has -25 and
not -26 (M26_BASE, default ci_pre26 — tests/ci.sh leaves it), with TEMP
revoked, and runs the script that owns the named check:

  battery check (V C A E F H)          battery-26.sql
  CR1-CR8                              races/collections-26.sh
  APPLY:<phrase>                       the patch itself must refuse, with that phrase

CAUGHT only when the FIRST failure printed is the named check's (the -21
rule). A mutation an EARLIER check catches is reported as missed: its named
check is wrong, or a guard is redundant — fix the name or remove the
duplicate, never loosen the rule.

    python3 tests/v5.4.2-26/mutations-26.py [--anchors] [M01 M12 ...]
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Callable

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
PATCH = Path(os.environ.get("PATCH26") or ROOT / "sql/v5.4.2-26-collections-decisions.sql")
HERE = ROOT / "tests/v5.4.2-26"
BASE = os.environ.get("M26_BASE", "ci_pre26")
DB = "m26"


def rep(old: str, new: str) -> Callable[[str], str]:
    def m(s: str) -> str:
        assert s.count(old) == 1, f"mutation anchor not unique/found ({s.count(old)}): {old[:90]!r}"
        return s.replace(old, new, 1)
    return m


def tail(sql: str) -> Callable[[str], str]:
    """Append statements just before the patch's own invariant run."""
    return rep("DO $$ BEGIN\n    PERFORM public.assert_collections_guard_invariants();",
               sql + "\nDO $$ BEGIN\n    PERFORM public.assert_collections_guard_invariants();")


# Anchors that occur more than once carry the message line that follows them.
